<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  MK.37.H — the splash icon is the square MARK, not the wide lockup.

  MB-350 fixed a real bug here (the theme pointed at @drawable/ic_logo, the
  16:9 badge+wordmark lockup, and the splash API masks its icon to a CIRCLE, so
  both ends of a wide image were cut off). Its fix was to shrink the lockup
  until its diagonal fit the circle — 132x74dp inside a 160dp mask. That stopped
  the clipping and left the logo small and adrift in a large empty circle, which
  is what the owner asked to be redesigned.

  A round mask wants a round subject. `ic_logo_mark` is the 96x96 hexagon badge:
  square artwork, so it fills the visible circle instead of being inscribed in
  it, and nothing is cut off because there is nothing wide to cut.

  The wordmark is not lost — it moves to where it can actually be set as type.
  The system splash cannot show a wordmark and a progress indicator; it draws
  one masked icon and nothing else. `BrandSplash` draws both, in Compose, the
  moment the window is ours.

  The transparent 240dp square underneath is still load-bearing (MB-350's own
  finding): without it the drawable's intrinsic size is the bitmap's, and the
  platform scales the icon to fill the canvas — which would push the mark back
  out to the mask's edge. The square pins the intrinsic aspect to 1:1 so the
  inset survives. 108dp of a 160dp visible circle leaves the badge room to read
  as a badge rather than as a disc.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <size android:width="240dp" android:height="240dp" />
            <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
        </shape>
    </item>
    <item android:gravity="center">
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/ic_logo_mark"
            android:gravity="center"
            android:width="108dp"
            android:height="108dp" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
